          name:"Necessary vs Permissive Joinder",
          draft:false,
          rarity:"uncommon",
          intro:{ idea:"Some people must be in a lawsuit and some may be. A person who ought to be a party for complete relief, or who might be unfairly affected by the judgment, must be joined (necessary joinder). People whose claims or defenses come from the same transaction or occurrence may join voluntarily if a common question of law or fact would arise (permissive joinder). Leaving out someone who should be joined is a ground for dismissal without prejudice, unless the court lets the case go on. Wrongly joining someone is not a ground for dismissal.", example:"Two passengers are hurt in the same crash and each has a claim against the driver. They may join as plaintiffs in one action, because their claims arise from the same occurrence and share a common question. That is permissive joinder. A person whose absence would leave the court unable to give complete relief ought to be made a party. That is necessary joinder, and if that person is left out, the defendant may move to dismiss without prejudice.", source:"CPLR 1001, 1002, 1003" },
          source:{ cite:"CPLR 1001(a), 1002(a)-(b), 1003", quote:"CPLR 1001(a): Persons who ought to be parties if complete relief is to be accorded between the persons who are parties to the action or who might be inequitably affected by a judgment in the action shall be made plaintiffs or defendants. When a person who should join as a plaintiff refuses to do so he may be made a defendant.\n\nCPLR 1002(a): Persons who assert any right to relief jointly, severally, or in the alternative arising out of the same transaction, occurrence, or series of transactions or occurrences, may join in one action as plaintiffs if any common question of law or fact would arise.\n\nCPLR 1002(b): Persons against whom there is asserted any right to relief jointly, severally, or in the alternative, arising out of the same transaction, occurrence, or series of transactions or occurrences, may be joined in one action as defendants if any common question of law or fact would arise.\n\nCPLR 1003: Nonjoinder of a party who should be joined under section 1001 is a ground for dismissal of an action without prejudice unless the court allows the action to proceed without that party under the provisions of that section. Misjoinder of parties is not a ground for dismissal of an action. Parties may be added at any stage of the action by leave of court or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared, or once without leave of court within twenty days after service of the original summons or at anytime before the period for responding to that summons expires or within twenty days after service of a pleading responding to it. Parties may be dropped by the court, on motion of any party or on its own initiative, at any stage of the action and upon such terms as may be just. The court may order any claim against a party severed and proceeded with separately.", from:"NYSenate.gov Open Legislation (Laws of New York), official PDFs at legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/laws/CVP1001, CVP1002, CVP1003, checked 2026-10-09. May 31, 2026 exam-cutoff version and human review pending.", context:"Who must be in a lawsuit (1001), who may join (1002), and what happens when someone is left out or wrongly included (1003).", url:"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/CVP/1001" },
          hook:"MUST be joined: complete relief needs them, or the judgment would unfairly affect them. MAY join: the same transaction or occurrence plus a common question. Left out a MUST? Dismissal without prejudice, unless the court lets the case go on. Wrongly joined? Never a ground for dismissal.",
          lore:["Must is a need. May is a choice.", "Same event, common question: you may join.", "Leave out a must-join, and the case can be dismissed.", "Add one too many, and nothing falls."],
